+## 2. Independent verification of the ceiling-loss identity
+
+Source attacked: board #301 post `SoringP1 exec: exec-excess-boundedness-20261120`
+(2026-11-20T16:15:11Z). It claims, for a poset `P` with `e(P)` linear extensions,
+`b(P)=ceil(log2 e(P))`, `eps(P)=F(P)-b(P)`, and the minimax-worse adversary path
+`P_0,...,P_d`:
+
+    eps(P) = sum_i (1 - pi_i),   pi_i = b(P_{i-1}) - b(P_i),   d = b(P) + eps(P).
+
+and the sufficient Kraft-clean lemma: a step with both children `e <= 2^{b(parent)-1}`
+has `pi >= 1`, hence non-positive loss.
+
+Method: from-scratch Python (`/tmp/opencode/ceilingloss/verify.py`, no researcher code): labelled
+posets as transitively closed successor bitmasks; linear-extension count by subset DP; exact
+minimax `F` by memoisation; sorter picks the pair minimising the worse child's `F`, adversary
+takes a worse child. Enumeration reproduced A001035 counts `1,3,19,219,4231` for `n=1..5`.
+
+Result: **0 identity mismatches over all posets on n<=5** (1+3+19+219+4231 = 4473 states),
+`depth = b(e0)+eps` held throughout. No step had `pi >= 2` on any optimal adversary path;
+loss was always in `{0,1}`. Non-Kraft-clean steps nevertheless occurred often (108 steps over
+n=4 states, 5213 over n=5) while total loss `D(n)` is 0 for `n<=5`, confirming the
+Kraft-clean criterion is sufficient but plainly not necessary at the individual-step level.
+
+Transparency: my first script produced apparent mismatches; those were defects in my own
+checker (no dedup of generated posets, and following the globally largest-`F` child instead
+of the minimax-optimal pair's worse child). Both were corrected before the result above;
+the corrected counts match A001035. No board or repository claim was made from the buggy run.
+
+Consistency check on the companion tightness-propagation lemma (board post 2026-11-20T16:13:32Z):
+`L(15)=41`, `inc(16)=4=cl(16)`, and the literature value `S(15)=42` (Jing et al., cited in
+`docs/research-certificates-barriers.md`), so the lemma correctly does NOT force `S(16)=45`;
+this agrees with the published `S(16)=46`. No contradiction with known values found.
